"Measure the distance to nearby stars the way astronomers do, from the small shift in a star’s position as Earth goes around the Sun, and find out how far this method can reach.",
"<strong>Parallax.</strong> Hold a finger up at arm’s length and look at it with one eye, then the other: it jumps against the background. The closer the finger, the bigger the jump. Earth does the same thing for us on a much bigger scale: in January and July it is on opposite sides of the Sun, {#} AU apart, so a nearby star seems to shift against much more distant stars. Half of that shift is the star’s <strong>parallax</strong>, p: the angle the radius of Earth’s orbit ({#} AU) makes when seen from the star.",
"<strong>The parsec.</strong> Astronomers define the distance at which p is exactly {#}″ as one <strong>parsec</strong> (pc): {#} pc = {#} {#} AU = {#} light years. Because the angles are tiny, the distance is simply",
